erential/14040222/assets/4dc193655982bcb9b5281b20814402e85ba1aeda39769e256b74a95a47ba6db4.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd015bcdce88748b0ab869510589ee853438ce853467733016e8d8bfa70131a7"} {"question" "q-96167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd015bcdce88748b0ab869510589ee853438ce853467733016e8d8bfa70131a7"} {"question" "q-96167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/b3e3fd451b0ebb2b4eb614dd54f5193be179f8bdcf7d84191004ddb80e39b3f8.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line vary as we go left and right along the line. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/7715c9573d2a434bb5878ff3900632dae7121bd752bab6a9a7745f69de84f0ce.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd015bcdce88748b0ab869510589ee853438ce853467733016e8d8bfa70131a7"} {"question" "q-96167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "387d3f02a5663f54b107e68bcfa7987176dc6e06a6500c05ca757abc31eea180"} {"question" "q-96167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd015bcdce88748b0ab869510589ee853438ce853467733016e8d8bfa70131a7"} {"question" "q-96167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/28e3f04c968be6ac105e060c999c4a12da00fbf5a8b4adadb05a515605c16ba1.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/950dfcf38eeb71cc51b1c16d02a31d0a4185ecb60bc3234a0c3314b2ab79e5b1.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/a69e37e076aded0627990fcb2d6a3c5e1bc2935ffc6337fee0cc7443c3fd17c6.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/b3e3fd451b0ebb2b4eb614dd54f5193be179f8bdcf7d84191004ddb80e39b3f8.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/edd7d0ec76f35b887b35ece9ed1fb7d5e6c881e5a4e2a9f33b4d065fab7ee848.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd015bcdce88748b0ab869510589ee853438ce853467733016e8d8bfa70131a7"} {"question" "q-96167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/b3e3fd451b0ebb2b4eb614dd54f5193be179f8bdcf7d84191004ddb80e39b3f8.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd015bcdce88748b0ab869510589ee853438ce853467733016e8d8bfa70131a7"} {"question" "e-8502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/2d6f4a939a2c25d1706a7d54a323af2773d30ddb08bcfcada94466bb8d1b9ef9.png)\n\nSketch the slope field of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y-1$ at the points indicated in the diagram above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/example-8502.json" "file_sha256" "074360d48621d628689d48c8012df392d4c762e44467568c753c90a4cef0db77"} {"question" "e-8502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(2,1), (-2,1),$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=2(1)-1=1 \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=2(1)-1=1 \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,2)}=2(2)-1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/be6bc036e194683af09a5936d433d7d45c7532e06054d9d473f3b433167ea102.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/example-8502.json" "file_sha256" "074360d48621d628689d48c8012df392d4c762e44467568c753c90a4cef0db77"} {"question" "e-8503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3y-1$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y = -1,$ the slope of the slope field does not vary\n\n- At every point along the line $x = 0,$ the slope of the slope field varies\n\n- At every point along the line $y = 0,$ the slope field is horizontal" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/example-8503.json" "file_sha256" "487ea7e19519b4b20bfad79d40f33e1ef5cbf8b049102a907bea7dbc8d3321aa"} {"question" "e-8503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,-1)} = 3(-1)-1 = -4,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-4$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(0,y)} = 3y-1,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(0,y)$ depends on $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,0)} = 3(0)-1=-1 \\neq 0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is $-1$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/f3ca77530d9126d733d33dc01ccee82d2740f789dcf712c67f03139fb09b753b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/example-8503.json" "file_sha256" "487ea7e19519b4b20bfad79d40f33e1ef5cbf8b049102a907bea7dbc8d3321aa"} {"question" "e-8504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/3990c49349d446512d59f1f722506ea7337143eba6ec49f29dc87af060fb553b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/example-8504.json" "file_sha256" "def2c22eb901e23ae6f7dff0f66e836f129f824662920b378ff03b1945bde461"} {"question" "e-8504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/df3dd6c7db3e88ac2ac2a0ad82b19e5cac427f69ddf0eeba841445612e62ec4e.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we move left and right along the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/4d518885f30e40ed870dba7b902139fca6ccbde81f7e468631c5199e887c4923.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/example-8504.json" "file_sha256" "def2c22eb901e23ae6f7dff0f66e836f129f824662920b378ff03b1945bde461"}] "usage" {"q-96167" [] "e-8502" [] "q-97622" [] "e-8503" [] "e-8504" []} "basis" 1421 "retractions" [[17592186172582 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = y^{2} - y$?"] [17592186172582 :question/worked-solution "Use $y'=y^2-y$. At the two points with $y=1$, the slope is $1-1=0$, so both segments are horizontal. At the point with $y=-2$, the slope is $4+2=6$, a steep positive slope. Select the diagram with those three slopes."] [17592186172560 :question/problem "Which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = f(y)$?"] [17592186172560 :question/worked-solution "For an autonomous equation $y'=f(y)$, every point on a fixed horizontal line must have the same slope. The selected diagram has slopes that change as $x$ changes along a horizontal line; therefore it cannot represent $y'=f(y)$. The other diagrams keep the slopes constant across each horizontal row."] [17592186172560 :question/difficulty 17592186045463] [17592186171771 :question/problem "Sketch the slope field of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y-1$ at the points indicated in the diagram above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/fa81fd3beb39de6abd0fa2d7b68f9194.png)"] [17592186171771 :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(2,1), (-2,1)$, and $(-2,2)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.y'\\right|_{(2,1)}&=2(1)-1=1,\\\\\n\\left.y'\\right|_{(-2,1)}&=2(1)-1=1,\\\\\n\\left.y'\\right|_{(-2,2)}&=2(2)-1=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/1f61f2a6d4602e7660245b212c064045.png)"] [17592186171772 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3y-1$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point along the line $y = -1$, the slope of the slope field does not vary\n2. At every point along the line $x = 0$, the slope of the slope field varies\n3. At every point along the line $y = 0$, the slope field is horizontal"] [17592186171772 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y$, the slope does not vary with $x$.\n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,-1)} = 3(-1)-1 = -4$,\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-4$ for any $x$.\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(0,y)} = 3y-1$,\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(0,y)$ depends on $y$.\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,0)} = 3(0)-1=-1 \\neq 0$,\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is $-1$ for any $x$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/23f047df3d0fa3e0d11f9be81ec6d279.png)"] [17592186171773 :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/ce8ff84e785ad9edbbd56ef02d4e0ed8.png)"] [17592186171773 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y$, the slope does not vary with $x$.\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/fe3aa4398d5f839f6c68b1b1a7fa3b5d.png)\n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we move left and right along the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/3ac23747a88c5d438abce91c471b6228.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186171326 17592186171327 17592186171328 17592186171329 17592186171330 17592186171458 17592186171459 17592186171460 17592186171461 17592186171462] "immutable_fields" [[17592186171325 :answer-field/correct] [17592186171325 :answer-field/id] [17592186171325 :answer-field/key] [17592186171325 :answer-field/type] [17592186171457 :answer-field/correct] [17592186171457 :answer-field/id] [17592186171457 :answer-field/key] [17592186171457 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
